      en: "Wasgamuwa National Park, established in 1984, covers 370 km² in Sri Lanka's dry zone. It is part of the elephant corridor and has a significant elephant population, estimated at 150–200. The park includes diverse habitats such as dry monsoon forests, grasslands, and wetlands. It is home to many bird species, including the endemic Sri Lanka junglefowl and rare fish-eating eagles. The Mahaweli River flows through the park, providing water for wildlife. The area has been historically used for shifting cultivation, which has shaped the vegetation.",
